course: books
lesson: 3
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter II, part 1: Curiouser and curiouser (who am I?)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The cake works: Alice shoots up like a pulled-out telescope until her feet seem miles away, and she jokes about posting them presents for Christmas.
explain: Her head bumps the roof of the hall, she is more than nine feet tall, and now she can only peep at the garden with one eye, so she cries and cries until a pool of tears spreads half down the hall.
explain: The White Rabbit comes back, dressed up, with white kid gloves and a fan, muttering about the Duchess; when giant Alice says "If you please, sir", he drops everything and runs off in fright.
explain: Fanning herself, Alice wonders whether she has been changed in the night into another girl, tests herself with times tables, geography and a poem, and gets them all wrong.
explain: She fears she has become Mabel, a girl she thinks knows very little, and ends up crying because she is so tired of being all alone: funny, but lonely and sad underneath.
fact: Alice cries "Curiouser and curiouser!", which is not good English (the right form is "more and more curious"), because she is so surprised she forgets how to speak properly.
fact: She says "Good-bye, feet!" because her feet are getting so far off, and plans to give them "a new pair of boots every Christmas", sent by the carrier (a delivery man).
fact: She imagines the address: "Alice's Right Foot, Esq., Hearthrug, near the Fender"; "Esq." (Esquire) is a polite title on letters to a gentleman, the hearthrug is the rug in front of the fireplace and the fender is the low metal guard around it.
fact: Her head strikes the roof of the hall: she is now "more than nine feet high", so she takes the golden key and hurries to the garden door.
fact: Lying on one side she can only look into the garden with one eye, so getting through is "more hopeless than ever".
fact: She tells herself "You ought to be ashamed of yourself, a great girl like you", a joke because she really is great, meaning huge, and orders herself: "Stop this moment, I tell you!", but goes on crying all the same.
fact: She sheds "gallons of tears", making a pool about four inches deep that reaches half down the hall.
fact: The White Rabbit returns "splendidly dressed", with white kid gloves (soft gloves made of young goat's leather) and a large fan, worried the Duchess will be "savage" (furious) if kept waiting.
fact: When Alice timidly says "If you please, sir—", the Rabbit drops the gloves and fan and scurries away into the darkness; Alice picks them up and, "as the hall was very hot", keeps fanning herself.
fact: Alice wonders, "Who in the world am I? Ah, that's the great puzzle!", and thinks of girls her age she might have been changed into.
fact: She is sure she is not Ada, whose hair "goes in such long ringlets" (long hanging curls), and fears being Mabel, who "knows such a very little".
fact: Her sums go wrong: "four times five is twelve, and four times six is thirteen" (in Alice's day the times tables at school stopped at twelve times), and her geography too: "London is the capital of Paris".
fact: The poem she tries to recite comes out as "How doth the little crocodile", about a crocodile that smiles as it welcomes little fishes into its jaws; "How doth" is old English for "how does".
fact: Alice thinks Mabel lives in a "poky little house" (small and cramped) with next to no toys and "ever so many lessons to learn", and these are only Alice's guesses about another girl; if she is Mabel, she says, she will stay down here, even if people put their heads down and call "Come up again, dear!"
fact: In the end she bursts into tears: "I do wish they would put their heads down! I am so very tired of being all alone here!"
exercise: What did Alice cry out when she began to grow? | "Curiouser and curiouser!" | "Oh dear! I shall be late!" | "Off with her head, at once!" | "Drink me! Eat me! Open me!"
exercise: Why did Alice say "Curiouser and curiouser"? | She was so surprised she forgot how to speak good English | She was copying a word the White Rabbit had used before | It was the proper grammar in the days when the book was written | She was trying to make her sister laugh up on the bank
exercise: Why did Alice say "Good-bye, feet!"? | They were getting so far away as she grew | They had run off down the passage by themselves | She was taking off her shoes to have a swim | She was going to post them home for Christmas
exercise: What did Alice plan to give her feet every Christmas? | A new pair of boots | Some warm woolly socks | A big box of sweet comfits | A shiny little golden key
exercise: In "Alice's Right Foot, Esq., Hearthrug, near the Fender", what is the fender? | The low metal guard around a fireplace | A rug that sits in front of the fire | A polite name for a gentleman | The man who carries parcels to houses
exercise: What does "Esq." stand for? | Esquire, a polite title for a gentleman | Especially, meaning more than usual | Escape, meaning get away quickly | Equal, meaning the same as something else
exercise: How tall was Alice when her head hit the roof? | More than nine feet | About ten inches | About two feet | About four feet and a bit
exercise: What did Alice take as soon as she was tall? | The little golden key | The bottle marked "DRINK ME" | The White Rabbit's fan | A big piece of mushroom
exercise: How much of the garden could the giant Alice see? | Only a view through the door with one eye | The whole garden from the top of the table | Nothing at all, since the curtain hid it | Just the tops of the trees over a high wall
exercise: How deep was the pool of tears? | About four inches | About four feet deep | About forty inches | About one inch or less
exercise: How far did the pool of tears reach? | Half down the hall | Out into the garden | Right up to the ceiling | Just round her feet
exercise: What was the Rabbit carrying when it came back? | A pair of white kid gloves and a large fan | A watch, a key and a little glass bottle | A big black umbrella and a cup of hot sweet tea | A basket of carrots and a long ladder
exercise: What are kid gloves? | Gloves made of soft leather from young goats | Gloves made just for small children | Gloves knitted out of rabbit fur | Gloves that are too small for anyone
exercise: Who was the Rabbit afraid of keeping waiting? | The Duchess | The Queen of Hearts | The girl Alice | His own housemaid
exercise: Here "savage" means... | Very angry | Very wild and hairy | Very hungry | Very late indeed
exercise: What did the Rabbit do when Alice spoke to it? | Dropped the gloves and fan and ran away | Bowed low and asked her name politely | Gave her the key and told her the way | Laughed at her rudely and called her a silly giant
exercise: What did Alice do with the fan? | Fanned herself, as the hall was hot | Threw it after the running Rabbit | Used it to scoop up the pool of her tears | Hid it under the glass table
exercise: What is "the great puzzle" Alice talks about? | Who in the world she is | How to get into the garden | Why the Rabbit ran off so fast | Where the golden key has gone
exercise: Why was Alice sure she was not Ada? | Ada's hair went in long ringlets and hers did not | Ada was much older and taller than Alice | Ada could not read or write at all | Ada had a cat called Dinah just like hers
exercise: What did Alice say four times five was? | Twelve | Twenty | Nine | Fifteen
exercise: What did Alice say London was the capital of? | Paris | Rome | England | Mabel
exercise: Which animal was the poem Alice recited about? | A crocodile | A busy bee | A white rabbit | A little fish
exercise: In the poem, what does the crocodile do with little fishes? | Welcomes them in with gently smiling jaws | Teaches them how to swim in the river Nile | Helps them home when they are lost | Polishes their shining golden scales
exercise: Here "How doth" means... | How does | How did | How do they | How dare
exercise: What did Alice imagine about Mabel's home? | A poky little house with next to no toys | A big castle full of lessons and books | A cosy cottage by the seaside with a cat | A palace where the Duchess lived as well
exercise: Thinking back to the start: what did Alice see the Rabbit take out of its waistcoat-pocket? | A watch | A key | A fan | A glove
exercise: Thinking back: what made Alice grow so tall in this part? | The cake marked "EAT ME" | The bottle marked "DRINK ME" | The golden key | The marmalade jar
test: Ruby goes: "Alice was crying 'cos the Rabbit was mean to her." Put her right. | She cried because she was too big for the garden door | She cried because the Rabbit called her names | She cried because the Rabbit took away the golden key | She cried because her sister had gone home
test: Darnell, young Gen Z: "Rabbit saw giant Alice and dipped, no cap." ("dipped" = left in a hurry, "no cap" = for real.) What did the Rabbit leave behind? | Its white kid gloves and fan | Its watch and waistcoat | The little golden key | A letter for the Duchess
test: Why is it funny when Alice calls herself "a great girl like you"? | "Great" means big, and she really is nine feet tall | She is a very clever girl who knows everything | She always calls herself great when she is happy | It is what the White Rabbit said about her
test: What would have happened if Alice had stayed ten inches high and still had the key? | She could have gone through the little door | She would have grown to nine feet anyway | The Rabbit would have taken the key again | She would have fallen into the pool of tears
test: Freya from Newcastle: "Howay, she knows nowt, saying London's the capital of Paris!" ("howay" = come on, "nowt" = nothing.) Why does Alice get things wrong here? | She is upset and muddled, and fears she has changed | She never went to school and learnt nothing | She is making jokes on purpose for the Rabbit | The book is set in a land where London is in France
test: True or false: Alice thinks she might have been changed for another child in the night. | True: she wonders if she has become Mabel | False: she never doubts who she is at all | True: she thinks she has become the Duchess | False: she thinks the Rabbit changed into her
test: Nan, firm: "Don't you talk like that about poor Mabel, she can't help her house." Which is true in the book? | Alice only guesses that Mabel's house is poky | Alice has been to Mabel's house many times | Mabel lives in a palace with lots of toys | Mabel is a mouse who lives down the well
test: Why does Alice decide that if she is Mabel she will stay down the hole? | She does not want to live Mabel's life with lots of lessons | She loves the hall and wants to live in it for ever | The Rabbit has told her that she can never ever go back up | Her sister has said she must not come home again
test: What does Alice really want at the end of this part? | For people to put their heads down and call her | To be left alone in the hall for ever and ever | To become as tall as the roof of the hall | To find Mabel and swap places with her again
test: Looking back to the fall: what did Alice once say she knew about the earth that she could not use? | Words like Latitude and Longitude | How to swim in very deep water | The way to the Rabbit's house | Where Dinah hides her saucer
test: Looking back: in part 2, Alice liked "pretending to be two people". What is she unsure of now? | Whether she is even one person, herself | Whether she has a big sister up there at all | Whether the Rabbit is two rabbits | Whether Dinah is really a cat
test: Looking back over the hall: what has Alice now been, size by size? | Normal, then ten inches, then over nine feet | Normal, then nine feet, then ten inches | Ten inches, then her normal size, then nine feet | Nine feet, then normal, then ten inches
test: Tom, rough: "Stop blubbing, you big baby, it's only a door." ("blubbing" = crying noisily.) What does Alice say to herself? | "Stop this moment, I tell you!" | "Curiouser and curiouser!" | "Oh my ears and whiskers!" | "Drink me, eat me!"
test: ★★★ Alice says "four times five is twelve, and four times six is thirteen". If each answer goes up by one, what does she get for four times twelve, where the old times tables stopped? | Nineteen | Twenty | Sixteen | Forty-eight
test: ★★★ That is why she says she will "never get to twenty at that rate". Why never? | The table stops at twelve before she reaches twenty | Twenty is not a real number in Wonderland at all | She forgets the numbers after thirteen | Four times anything can never make twenty
test: ★★★ Alice went from ten inches to more than nine feet. Nine feet is 108 inches. About how many times taller did she get? | About eleven times | About nine times | About twice | About a hundred times
exam: Identify the reason for which the protagonist uttered the expression "Curiouser and curiouser". | Her surprise caused her temporarily to forget correct English | The expression constitutes correct grammar of that period | She wished to imitate the speech of the White Rabbit | She intended to amuse her sister upon the riverbank
exam: Ascertain the approximate depth of the pool formed by the tears of the protagonist. | Four inches | Four feet | Nine inches | Nine feet
exam: It is given that the protagonist addressed the White Rabbit with the words "If you please, sir". Determine the consequence thereof. | The Rabbit relinquished its gloves and fan and fled | The Rabbit surrendered the key of the little door | The Rabbit escorted her forthwith to the Duchess | The Rabbit dried her tears with its pocket-handkerchief
exam: Select the response most consistent with the established facts concerning the protagonist's recitation of verse. | The words emerged altered, concerning a crocodile | The words were recited in perfect correctness | The verse was recited in its entirety in the French language | The verse concerned the White Rabbit's watch
